lesson k-what-soaks-up-water v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=2 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Wet and dry"
  goal "Say which things soak up water and which keep it out, and what makes wet things dry."

  look {
    scene 19x10 {
      materials row things=["towel", "raincoat", "paper-bag", "plastic-bottle"] letters=1 names=1 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      Pour water on a towel and it soaks in. Pour water on a raincoat and it runs off, so the coat
      keeps you dry. Paper soaks water up, and plastic does not. Wet washing dries when the water
      goes off into the air, fastest on a warm, windy day.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.soaks-or-not k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.soaks-or-not count=3 seed=2 easy-count=2
    practice k.count-soakers count=2 seed=3
    practice k.which-keeps-dry count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    show k.what-dries k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.why-a-towel
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.water-on-top k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Some things soak water up. Others keep it out."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. A tray, a jug of water and a few things from the kitchen make this a
    test the child can do: guess first, then pour. If the child is stuck, let them feel a wet towel
    and a wet plastic bag. Off the page, peg out washing together and check it every hour. Japan,
    幼稚園教育要領 (kindergarten guidelines), 環境, 内容 (2): "生活の中で，様々な物に触れ，その性質や仕組みに興味や関心をもつ。"
    (Touch many things in daily life and take an interest in how they behave and work.)
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the towel is worked in the teacher's pen first, the things to judge are a towel, a cup and a sponge, and the washing is the drying question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the things to judge include paper and glass, the rows are harder to count, and the drying question asks where the water goes."
  }
}
